  "text": "Allophone: A variant of a phoneme that occurs in a specific phonetic environment.\n\nArticulation: The physical process of producing speech sounds through the movement of speech organs.\n\nDialect: A regional or social variety of a language distinguished by vocabulary, grammar, and pronunciation.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound in which the tongue moves from one position to another during the utterance of a single syllable.\n\nEjective Consonant: A sound produced by compressing air in the pharynx and then releasing it abruptly.\n\nFormant: A resonance frequency of the vocal tract that distinguishes one vowel from another.\n\nGrammar: The system of rules governing the structure of sentences in a language.\n\nHaplology: The omission of a syllable or morpheme in a word due to phonological simplification.\n\nIntonation: The melody or pitch pattern in a sentence, which can indicate emotion or sentence type.\n\nLabial Sound: A sound produced with the lips, such as /p/, /b/, or /m/.\n\nLaryngeal Sound: A sound produced by vibrations of the vocal folds, such as vowels and some consonants.\n\nLexicon: The vocabulary of a language, including words and their meanings.\n\nMorpheme: The smallest meaningful unit in a language, such as \"un-\" in \"unhappy.\"\n\nMinimal Pair: A pair of words that differ by only one phoneme, such as \"pat\" and \"bat.\"\n\nMonophthong: A single, pure vowel sound, as opposed to a diphthong.\n\nNasal Consonant: A sound produced with the airflow passing through the nose, such as /m/ or /n/.\n\nObstruent: A consonant made with a forced or stopped airflow, such as stops, fricatives, and affricates.\n\nOversimplification: A common error in language learning where complex rules are simplified incorrectly.\n\nPhoneme: The smallest unit of sound in a language that can distinguish meaning.\n\nPhonetics: The study of the sounds of speech, including their production, transmission, and perception.\n\nPhonology: The study of the sound system of a language, including the rules governing sound patterns.\n\nProsody: The intonation, stress, and rhythm of speech.\n\nProto-language: A reconstructed ancestral language from which modern languages are believed to have developed.\n\nReciprocal Pronoun: A pronoun used to refer to two or more people who are performing an action to each other.\n\nRetroflex Consonant: A consonant produced with the tongue curled back toward the roof of the mouth.\n\nRoot: The base form of a word or a morpheme that carries the core meaning.\n\nSibilant: A consonant that involves a hissing sound, such as /s/ or /z/.\n\nStress: The emphasis placed on a syllable within a word or phrase, usually indicated by pitch and loudness.\n\nSuprasegmental: Features of speech that operate above the level of individual sounds, such as stress and intonation.\n\nSyntax: The set of rules that govern the structure of sentences in a language.\n\nTone: A pitch pattern that can function as a phonological feature in some languages, such as in tonal languages.\n\nUvular Consonant: A consonant produced with the back of the tongue touching the uvula, such as /r/ in some languages.\n\nVowel: A sound produced without any obstruction of the airflow in the vocal tract.\n\nVelar Consonant: A consonant produced with the back of the tongue touching the soft palate, such as /k/ or /g/.\n\nWord Formation: The process by which new words are created from existing words or root forms.\n\nZero Derivation: The creation of a new word from an existing word without adding",
